  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_call_7 = "Venerable PlayerName,",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_1 = "Cute Little Caelestite PlayerName,\n\nI haven't seen you in so long. I hope you're doing alright.\n\nSurprisingly, it was Illumina's little birdie that delivered your letter to me. I thought she'd be too busy with all that goes on during Mooncrown Day festivities. In fact, I assumed that she must have gotten her recipient mixed up again, but this time I was really surprised. I haven't received a letter from anyone in years, let alone on Mooncrown Day. That's why it felt so unreal receiving such a heavy package from you. When I opened it, I found a beautiful, special bar of Gemstone Chocolate inside, and it was rich dark chocolate—my favorite! Silky and mellow, with the aroma of malt and the slight astringency of fruit. Perfect for a lazy afternoon alone at the office in the Courier Union. You're so thoughtful, little Caelestite. \n\nAs I write this letter, I can't help but break off another chunk to savor. You have such good taste, PlayerName. Even the way this chocolate is packaged is so dazzling and resplendent. Speaking of, I don't know if you are familiar with the habits of desert dwellers. Maybe it's because of the climate and geography of the desert, but most people don't seem to like sweet things. However, I've loved them since I was a child. I'm not addicted, but I do enjoy them more than most. Once when I was young, I saw a child eating chocolate for the first time on the Colossus. He said that it was the sweetest thing on the whole continent. I didn't make friends with anyone back then, so I just watched him from a distance. But actually, I wanted it so badly that I began to cry. \n\nThe funny thing is, I couldn't sleep that night. I had heard of people going to desert oases to make sugar from insect honey. They called this desert candy. I couldn't bear it—I ran off to the desert myself to search for bugs. I was really lucky, in hindsight. If I had encountered Eclipsites or sandstorms back then, I'm afraid I wouldn't be sitting here replying to your letter now. Anyways, I continued until I couldn't feel my legs anymore. Unexpectedly and miraculously, I did end up finding an oasis. That was the first time I saw a real oasis. The Rediesel Desert was always bright and full of stars; that night, it was brighter than ever. Moonlight caressed the sand dunes, its silver beams spilling over the contours of the desert as if flowing into the sky. Starlight cascaded quietly to the earth in the ephemeral twilight, and was reflected in a small body of water at the oasis. The colorful reflection was truly a sight to behold; I was completely stunned. Can you understand the wonder I must've felt back then, as a child who didn't know anything of the world? Considering the experiences you've had, I think you know the feeling well, little Caelestite. \n\nI found a little bit of honey there and sat on the edge of the star-speckled water, suckling the sweet flavor beneath the moonlight. To this day, I still think it's the sweetest thing I have ever eaten in my life, a taste that no sweet treat could ever match. It was noon the next day when my parents and the other adults found me. They said that I'd been sleeping at the edge of the oasis with a silly smile on my face. Nobody could wake up, which frightened them. \n\nIn retrospect, my experience that night may have been what first sparked my desire to leave the desert and see the world. I have now seen all kinds of places, but that moonlit night will always be the most beautiful memory in my heart. It would be wonderful if, by describing it to you in this letter, I can truly share the sights I beheld that night with you. \n\nAlthough I must admit, Gemstone Chocolate is also very delicious. I think it might just be as sweet as the honey I had under the galactic nights back then.",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_2 = "Dearest PlayerName,\n\nI haven't heard from you in a long time! \n\nTo be honest, I was a little surprised when I received a package from Dove. I'm usually the one delivering letters to others, and this was the first time I received a package of my own. I never thought I'd receive a gift from you for Mooncrown Day... I'm so happy! This is cool, mint chocolate, isn't it? It's so nice being able to have something so yummy after a long day of deliveries! Thank you so much, PlayerName! \n\nWhen I received your package, I had just sent an emergency delivery to a remote village in Northland. Like my hometown of Faar, it was a small place super far from the Winter Palace. The path over was rugged and often blocked by heavy snow; it took me several days to deliver the package. \n\nOnce I arrived, I discovered that the recipient was a little girl with a rare disease. She needed to take medicine every day to stay healthy. But these meds can't be found in Northland, so she has to buy it from Illumina on a regular basis and have it sent by mail. Her parents are always worried that the courier can't deliver on time due to how difficult the journey is to where they live. Also, due to obvious health concerns, the little girl has never left Northland. \n\nShe reminds me of myself when I was a child. I was also born in a remote village, and the only way I could learn about the outside world was through the couriers who would deliver letters to us from time to time. But I was more fortunate, of course. I was healthy, and could choose to leave Northland and become a courier, to act as a bridge between people in remote villages like mine and the rest of Astra. \n\nAnyways, I also wanted to apologize to you. Making this chocolate must've taken up a lot of your time. The sweetness of the white chocolate and the coolness of the mint filling are in perfect proportion. I know that probably took a long time to perfect. But you see, I decided to share it with the sick little girl without asking you first. \n\nShe'd never seen the outside world and didn't even know what I'd been holding in my hand. I told her it was chocolate, a sweet snack made by a good Caelestite friend of mine named PlayerName who flies around the world in a Colossus that looks like a huge dragon. After I told her that, she kept shooting furtive glances at the chocolate in my hand, yet didn't speak up. I figured she must've wanted to try this exotic food very badly, but was too shy to say so, seeing as how I was basically a stranger. That's why I decided to share some of the chocolate with her after confirming with her parents that eating it wouldn't have any adverse effects on her health.\n\nAfter eating a piece, the little girl finally smiled. She said that your chocolate was sweet, but also cold like snowflakes, and that from its taste, she could tell that the person who made it must be very gentle. When she gets well, she wants to meet you, meet your dragon-like Colossus, and travel around Astra like you. \n\nI told her that wouldn't be a problem and that I would tell you in person. And I also said that we could all travel together when she recovers. \n\nThank you, PlayerName. Thank you for your chocolate, and thank you for being my friend. \n\nBy the way, you gave me such wonderful chocolate, but I have nothing to give you in return... so I'm sending some unused stamps with this letter—next time you want to send a letter, you can use them. I'm looking forward to your reply!\n\nWishing you all the best!",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_3 = "PlayerName:\n\nIs this the right format for a letter? I haven't written to anyone in such a long time. Anyways, PlayerName, I hope you've been doing well! Thank you for the chocolate! \n\nWhen that courier named Dove came to deliver it, she kept muttering to herself, asking whether or not she had delivered it to the wrong person. Does the Courier Union really make mistakes so often? She also specifically asked Chandra to help confirm the order, but it was obviously for me! If it weren't for her reminding me, I wouldn't have realized that it was already Mooncrown Day, and that it was from my favorite teammate, PlayerName. \n\nI must say, when I opened the package, I was really surprised—you know me so well, PlayerName! The game trophy packaging was genius. It looks exactly like the ones from my favorite game. I didn't even want to open it! That delicious, sweet milk chocolate was so silky and soft in my mouth. I almost ate the whole box without thinking while I was gaming, though I remembered to save a piece for Unimet. After all, she's the one who enlightened me in the ways of chocolate. \n\nAh, how did it happen again? I think that was when she first came to the research lab. I thought her inventions were very interesting from the start, but she was so scatterbrained, it was hard to hold an actual conversation with her. I was supposed to go home to play games that day, but I heard a little girl sobbing in the corridor. I wasn't interested in finding out what the matter was. After all, it had nothing to do with me. But when I went out and passed by her, I noticed Unimet squatting there, huddled in the corner. See, my gaming experience told me that if I spoke to her then, I'd be able to build rapport with her. So, I went over to pat her on the shoulder with the intention of gaining her favor. Her eyes nearly popped out with fear when she saw me, it was absolutely priceless! Eventually, after what felt like forever, I finally learned that she was under tremendous pressure in the research lab. She didn't know how to balance her relationship with her colleagues and was afraid to offend anyone. And on top of that, the small mechanical arm she had worked so hard to put together suddenly started acting up that day, leaving her completely at a loss. I took a quick look for her and found that basic engineering occupied too large a proportion of the project, which obviously resulted in the heavy mechanical arm breaking down too quickly. So I helped her repair it with Luminatics. Although it was almost effortless, I'll never forget her starry-eyed expression! I couldn't help her with her relationship issues though, since that's her own business. But I still let her stay up late to play games with me. I found her to be pretty smart, and a natural at gaming! Anyway, I digress. The next day, she dawdled before me for a while before finally slipping these candies wrapped in foil into my hands. It was something called chocolate that her mother had sent her from home. I didn't remember tasting anything like it before, and I didn't expect it to be so delicious. I wonder if people in that long-ago era had something like this? But anyways, speaking as someone from the present, they're rightfully addicting.\n\nHowever, compared with the flavor of Unimet's old-fashioned Undercity chocolate, the chocolatey flavor of the stuff you sent me is so much richer, and the taste of the milk and nuts is so much more intense. Eating it makes me feel so energetic, like I could stay up all night! The next time you come over, PlayerName, I'll let you try some of that Undercity chocolate, and then we can play games while eating!",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_4 = [[
To the Caelestite, PlayerName,

Your package has been received. This is my reply. 

I received your letter, as well as the chocolate and teddy bear you sent me. The bear is cute, I like it... but unfortunately, I can't eat the chocolate, and can only look at it. They've been placed with the other items in my collection; I will look after them well. I have heard that raw chocolate goes bad very easily, so I asked a friend to seal it in ice with Luminatics, in the hopes that it will never deteriorate. The next time I come to the Colossus, I will bring this chocolate with me. However, the ice block is huge, so I'll probably need a giant box to hold it.
 
The last time I ate chocolate must have been quite a long time ago. I can't remember what it tastes like. I don't like to recall the past, so I don't care about the taste of chocolate. For me, it is more important to focus on the present. The price of being reborn and gaining the thundering power to defend order is losing part of one's ability to engage with the world in various ways, such as the sense of taste, and the ability to speak. I thank Raphael for saving me, and I don't regret exchanging these things for power.

Perhaps it's because I lost part of my ability to interact with the world that I began to take an interest in hoarding interesting objects... This may be a form of compensation for being disconnected from everything. Although Lumopolis is a city of strict order, solemnity, and tranquility, a city in the sky is still too far away from the ground below. Coming on the Colossus and traveling with you has finally allowed me to see the finer details of the world. When the Colossus flies high through the raging wind, I feel as though I am transformed into lightning, accompanying it as it flies over mountains and rivers, towns and villages. At the same time, I also find many interesting things I want to collect, such as Illumina's train, discarded Colossus parts, Umbraton's music boxes... ah, and the bear you gifted me.

The little bear is so wonderful to the touch. When I stroke its furry little head, it's just like touching a real animal. It's also extremely soft to squeeze and so different from the teddy bear I collected before. That other bear is very old. It has a lot of mending stitches, and the cotton filling is all shriveled. Unlike the one you gave me, that one isn't springy.

This is a very new and strange feeling for me. When I touch this new bear, the thunder ringing in my mind and body for so long seems soothed and becomes gentler. It's strange how this little bear can suppress my ability and affect the thunder I control. PlayerName, is this because of Luminatics applied to it, or is it some kind of unknown ability you possess, a talent of the Caelestites, just like how only you can hear me? I can't figure it out... This is a phenomenon that does not conform to any known law and should be rectified, but there is no stated rule to signify that this is wrong... 

It's really difficult to understand. Perhaps I should not be so hasty to form hypotheses about this kind of thing but should carefully observe the phenomenon and try to understand its laws and principles. I will set this little bear down and observe it carefully until I reach a conclusion.

May the God of Light bless you, PlayerName.]],
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_5 = "Dear comrade, PlayerName,\n\nThank you for your chocolate!\n\nI actually received it yesterday, but I was busy with official business and didn't have time to sit down to deal with personal affairs until the evening. You must've researched my preferences in advance, because the gift is perfect. I'm elated, and very satisfied with the chocolate. Thank you for being so considerate. \n\nYou know what? Chocolate and coffee are sometimes rationed out to soldiers. Chocolate can quickly replenish your energy, while coffee can perk you up and keep you awake through the night. So, when I eat your chocolate, I can't help thinking about the past. \n\nI was just a teenager back then, and had only just stepped onto a real battlefield for the first time. At that time, I was an inexperienced rookie, like everyone else. I'd watched my comrades fall in that brutal war, my own futile attempts to fight back causing despair to well up within me. Though I could temporarily ignore the pain in the midst of battle, it would return to me once I was left alone with my thoughts. So, at dinner that day, I couldn't eat. Images of my fellow soldiers falling before me played over and over in my mind until everyone else had finished eating, and I still hadn't taken a bite. \nMaybe it was because I was in such a bad state, but when I packed up and got ready to leave, one of my comrades stopped me and gave me some chocolate. I wasn't in the mood to eat, but I couldn't resist—having gone hungry for a whole day, my stomach had begun to rumble. So, I pulled apart the packaging and reluctantly ate a small piece. Chocolate, as a military rations supplement, contains a lot of sugar, and tastes very sweet. This sweet taste melts on the tip of your tongue, soothing your anxiety. Without even realizing it, I was left with an empty wrapped in my hands, my hunger satiated. Isn't that incredible? A piece of plain old chocolate passed on the unyielding willpower of my comrades to me, and gave me the strength and courage to continue fighting the Eclipsites. \n\nThe taste of that chocolate still lingers on my tongue, but my old comrades and companions have long drifted away, taken by the vestiges of time. I miss them dearly. \n\nBut now, I have received this chocolate you sent me. Its bitter coffee taste and sweet aftertaste reminds me of the past, but also allows me to see the present for what it is—the new comrades and companions that stand by my side. The past is unforgettable, but it should not become a burden—it should become the force that drives me forward. Not to mention, you and everyone in the Illumina Federation are my comrades and companions right here and now, who will always support and strengthen me.\n\nI've written so much without even realizing it. I think the last time I spoke so freely to someone like this was before I became the Grand Marshal of the Illumina Federation. The next time you come to Illumina, let's talk further in person.\n\nMay the splendor of Illumina light up your path.",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_6 = "Dearest PlayerName,\n\nAlthough it was very unexpected, thank you for your chocolate. \n\nWhen Dove brought your package to the Rediesel Desert, I was shocked, mostly because she was panting with exhaustion while carrying a huge bag of mail taller than herself. I hurriedly asked her to sit down and rest, then asked her which package was for me, so that I could find it for myself. Dove said the entire bag of mail was for me—chocolate and letters from all over Astra. \n\nEvery Mooncrown Day, I receive heaps of chocolates and letters. At first, it was a major hassle, and I thought it was my unique constitution that was the root of the problem. I always ask Dove to return all the letters, then write them apology letters one by one, hoping that they'll stop sending me mail. And it still hasn't stopped... Every year, I need to spend ages writing all these letters of apology! But this year, when I asked Dove to send the mail back, she told me there was a package from you. That's the only reason I didn't return all the mail. \n\nI was a little worried when I found out that you had sent me chocolate. I thought my unique constitution had brainwashed you too, but after seeing the chocolate you sent, my worries disappeared. Because the chocolate you sent me was Allen Wrench-shaped, showing me just how well you know me. Sure, those who only care about my looks would've packed their chocolates and letters beautifully, but they wouldn't have considered my preferences at all. That's how I can be sure that you sent me this chocolate by your own volition, with a clear mind. \n\nAs this chocolate symbolizes your pure intentions, there's no way I could refuse it. Thank you so much, my friend! However, when the delivery arrived, I was still working on someone's car, so I had only the stationery I was using to write apology letters on hand. But writing to you on apology letter stationery would've made me feel bad, so I tore a page out of the notebook I use for my design sketches instead. I know this letter might not seem very formal, but I wanted to thank you for your sincerity. \n\nI'm so lucky to have been able to meet you and travel with you on your Colossus. There, I can observe the wonders of ancient technology up close, carry out my favorite research without interference, and be free of the fear of being visited by people who are drawn in by my special constitution. This truly is my dream life. Also, after meeting you and having a quiet research environment to work in, I've been able to turn more of my inspirations into actual machinery and, at the same time, become stronger. Even the Allen Wrench is glowing these days! \n\nYou've taken good care of me during the time we've spent together. Please let me continue working as a mechanic for you and Soroz! Whether it's fighting Eclipsites, repairing machinery, or maintaining the Colossus, I'm willing to offer you my services!\n\nI almost forgot that I recently obtained some high-quality parts that are super useful. Because they're extremely small, they're always getting lost, so I've made it a habit to always prepare extras. Let me give you some of these extraneous parts. You can use them to repair small machines like the Fireflies. Their effectiveness and durability are far superior to anything available on the market. Machines have feelings too, you know... If you treat them well, they'll return the favor. \nI hope these parts will be of help to you and your machinery!",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_info_7 = "Venerable PlayerName,\n\nHow are you? I hope you have been well recently. \n\nI received your package early in the morning. The professionalism and manners of the courier who sent it left much to be desired, and although she ran away after I gave her a little guidance, I think she still learned something. I hope that the Courier Union improves the etiquette of their messengers in the future. Her cloak looked as though it had not been cleaned for a long time... It was really quite inelegant. \n\nAfter she left, I busied myself preparing Miss Nina's breakfast. Since Miss Nina also needed me to help her finish making handmade chocolate for Mooncrown Day, quite some time passed before I was able to open your package that afternoon, PlayerName. I do apologize. But it surprised me that this elegant gift box decorated with gold foil contained chocolate truffles. Upon opening it, I suddenly found myself lost for words. I never imagined someone like me would ever receive chocolates as a gift. After hesitating for a moment, I ate one. It instantly melted in my mouth—the initial bitterness gradually gave way to a caramel and fruity taste. The base note was sweet and mellow. It really was the best chocolate I've ever had; ever indicative of your impeccable taste, PlayerName. I don't often indulge in treats like this, yet I couldn't help eating another piece.\n\nThe taste reminded me of the first time I ever ate chocolate. At that time, I was twelve or thirteen years old, but I didn't know what chocolate was, as I had lived a childhood of perpetual hunger and misery. It was not until I came under the guidance of my master and Master Androma, that I was able to learn how to live in this world normally, like a newborn learning to walk. One day, I found the mansion filled with copious amounts of flowers, silks, and small, delicate brown squares neatly stacked in the shape of little cakes. I had never seen such a thing before, yet I dared not ask any questions, so I just stared at them like a fool. Master sensed my bewilderment and told me that it was \"Mooncrown Day.\" The young mistress was going to invite her friends and hold a tea party for all the noble girls. Master and his wife were very happy that day, because their little girl was gradually growing up before their eyes. I watched the girls stir their silver teaspoons and place the brown squares in their mouths, which would cause happy expression to erupt on their faces. I couldn't help but be mesmerized.\n\nThat night, when I returned to my room, I found a small and exquisite gift box by my bedside. It looked so out of place in juxtaposition to the harshness of my life. I brought it to my master, but learned that during the day, Master Androma had noticed my stunned expression and assumed that I also wanted to eat those brown squares, so he'd saved some for me. That was when I learned that this food was called chocolate. I went back to my room and carefully unpacked the box. I placed a square carefully into my mouth, and was instantly shocked. I had never known, until that moment, that such delicious food existed in the world.\n\nSorry, I've been writing such nonsense; perhaps I'm boring you. PlayerName, the chocolate truffles you sent were so delicious, I couldn't help recalling those memories from so long ago. I will remember this sweetness you've bestowed upon me forever.",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_writter_1 = "Your desert friend, Jeriah",
  str_n27_valentine_y_letter_writter_2 = "Your friend, Kya",
